WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.912
Coding Exercise 6 applies what you learned about
JSONB filtering The task

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:09.288
is to return patient ID symptom and severity for
notes where follow_up

00:00:09.312 --> 00:00:14.575
is true Then sort the result by patient ID The
SELECT list

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:20.025
already extracts symptom and severity using the
arrow-arrow operator The missing part

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:24.888
is the WHERE condition Use the note column extract
the follow_up key

00:00:24.912 --> 00:00:28.275
as text cast the result to Boolean and compare it
with true

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:33.988
The pattern is note arrow-arrow follow_up then
double colon boolean equals true

00:00:34.862 --> 00:00:39.062
Keep follow_up inside single quotes because it is
the JSON key name

00:00:39.938 --> 00:00:44.438
After the WHERE clause the query should order by
patient ID Run

00:00:44.462 --> 00:00:48.725
and Check then inspect the rows The values shown in
symptom and

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.425
severity come from inside the JSONB note rather than
from separate relational

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:56.925
columns If the condition causes an error check the
placement of quotes

00:00:57.038 --> 00:01:02.550
parentheses and the Boolean cast JSON syntax can
look unfamiliar at first

00:01:02.812 --> 00:01:07.412
so compare your line carefully with the earlier
JSONB example This exercise

00:01:07.438 --> 00:01:12.250
shows that PostgreSQL can combine relational columns
and semi-structured JSON values in

00:01:12.275 --> 00:01:16.012
one query which is useful in many modern data
preparation workflows
